  "limitations": [
    "The test set is real-world imbalanced (about 15% scam), so F1 looks modest compared with balanced toy benchmarks. That is deliberate: it matches what a user actually receives.",
    "The SCAM threshold is floored at 0.55 (a false alarm is costly for the elderly users this app serves). That constraint lowers F1 slightly versus an unconstrained search — the cost is printed by the training script instead of hidden.",
    "TF-IDF beat the multilingual MiniLM embeddings on this corpus (short SMS-style text favours character n-grams, and the non-English training data is small). The embedding backend is still trained, published in the comparison table and selectable with TEXT_MODEL_BACKEND=embedding, but the default is the empirically better scorer — not the more fashionable one.",
    "Bangla, Hindi and Urdu coverage comes from 132 hand-reviewed synthetic rows; per-language metrics are shown separately because they are weaker than English. Treat non-English scores as indicative.",
    "The synthetic multilingual set was authored by the ScamLens team and is an imitation of documented fraud typologies — it is not intercepted real fraud traffic.",
    "The classifier recognises patterns present in its training data; novel scam wording, images and voice-only attacks can evade it. This is why the product shows a confidence value, explains the evidence, and never acts on the user's behalf.",
    "ScamLens flags fraud, not marketing. Legitimate bulk SMS is labelled legitimate, so some aggressive but legal promotions may still score in the SUSPICIOUS band."
  ],
  "notes": "ML-only = production text classifier. Ensemble = calibrated weighted mix of the ML score, the URL risk score and the pattern-library score, with a prior weight reserved for the LLM judgement. Weights and thresholds are tuned on the validation split only; the test split is scored once. Run ml/eval_llm.py with an API key to fill in the LLM-only column on the same test split.",
  "shipped_pipeline_eval": {
    "override_effect": {
